    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the customerCode collision de-duplication in `AssignCustomerCode.java`:\n\n- The base code `<REGION>-<HASH8>` is computed as before.\n- The step now also receives the `OwnerRepository` (injected by type, following the same pattern as `CheckIdentityUnique`) and collects all existing owners' non-null `customerCode` values.\n- If the base collides, it appends `-<n>` starting at `n=2` and increments until the value is unique, then assigns it \u2014 so the smallest `n \u2265 2` that de-duplicates wins (e.g. `NSW-1A2B3C4D`, then `NSW-1A2B3C4D-2`, `NSW-1A2B3C4D-3`, \u2026).\n\nDownstream steps (`AssignMembershipNumber`, `CustomerCodeCheckDigit`, `AuditOwnerCreated`, response `locality`) all read the now-de-duplicated `customerCode`, so they follow automatically. I also updated the class Javadoc to document the collision behavior.\n\nTests: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreCustomerCodesStayUnique`) asserts distinct owners always get distinct codes; note it can't force a genuine hash collision black-box (that needs an identical normalized telephone + last name, which telephone-uniqueness blocks), so the `-<n>` branch is exercised by the logic itself rather than a direct assertion \u2014 consistent with the test's own documented rationale.",
